WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
[MUSIC]

00:00:19.073 --> 00:00:22.586
Social media's just really recently
taken off as a marketing channel.

00:00:22.586 --> 00:00:27.173
So what we're seeing is that sort of on
surveys among CMOs, they say, right now,

00:00:27.173 --> 00:00:30.176
social media's about 10%
of marketing budgets.

00:00:30.176 --> 00:00:32.944
In 2018, it's projected to be 20%.

00:00:32.944 --> 00:00:36.641
We're seeing that increasing amounts of
marketing dollars are getting shifted

00:00:36.641 --> 00:00:39.803
from more traditional channels
towards social media more broadly.

00:00:39.803 --> 00:00:43.496
So this would involve both
advertising through social media,

00:00:43.496 --> 00:00:47.884
as well as firm trying to can a foster
consumers to talk about their brands by

00:00:47.884 --> 00:00:51.788
providing platform reviews,
by sometimes responding to them and

00:00:51.788 --> 00:00:54.460
does it a lot of ways which try to do it.

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:59.094
So seem that channel and if you ask CMO is
about 64% of CMO's is think word of mouth,

00:00:59.094 --> 00:01:04.440
online word of mouth is one of the most
powerful marketing channels in the future.

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:08.330
But at the same time,
it's actually not that well understood how

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:12.370
quantitatively strong these effects can
be, and that's where our study comes in.

00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:16.400
What we try to look for is, if you will,
an external shock to the system,

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:20.340
something that changed how much people
were talking about a particular product or

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:23.960
brand, that wasn't related to any
underlying characteristics of the brand.

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:27.570
So if we wanted to avoid us that more
people are talking about a brand because

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:29.244
more people bought it in the past.

00:01:29.244 --> 00:01:32.700
So essentially the talking about
is a result of the sales, but

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:36.890
we want to establish is that whether
talking can actually lead to more future

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:38.100
sales in that realm.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:38.998
And that's usually hard to do.

00:01:38.998 --> 00:01:44.152
[MUSIC]

00:01:44.152 --> 00:01:48.110
We look at Sina Weibo which is the Chinese
equivalent of Twitter if you will.

00:01:48.110 --> 00:01:49.470
A very similar type of platform.

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:53.150
Twitter itself is blocked in
Mainland China so it cannot be used.

00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:56.500
So Sina Weibo emerged as a substitute for
that.

00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:01.192
And what happened is that in 2012,
following a political scandal,

00:02:01.192 --> 00:02:03.820
Sina Weibo was partially blocked for
3 days and

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:07.390
so what we're going to investigating
is we're going to look at TV shows.

00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:09.500
A lot of them have
a very active following.

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:11.460
A lot of them are aired at a daily level,
so

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:15.480
we see every day people talk about these
episodes then they watch it at night.

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:20.110
Then suddenly for three days lot of this
activity is gone due to this external

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:23.100
influence, which is the censorship event
that's coming from the government.

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:27.030
And so essentially looking at that
something happened to show us that tended

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:30.340
to have a lot of following and suddenly
disappears during those three days,

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:33.210
do we see that being reflected
in their viewership numbers?

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:37.720
So the censorship event helped us a lot in
pinning something down that's otherwise

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:39.090
very hard to do.

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:40.620
So most studies prior to that.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:45.020
Usually it's something like
relating sales of a product to

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:50.850
the number of Tweets pertaining to the
product, maybe from the previous day or

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:54.520
week, something to assure that's
not happening at the same time.

00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:58.280
But even then, it's hard to know
whether that's the right attribution.

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:02.890
So you might imagine again, the example
that might be one high-quality product

00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:07.150
which constantly has a lot of sales and
constantly has a lot of Tweets about it.

00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:09.460
Anther product does not have that, and

00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:14.590
even if we look at temporal differences
between sales and the microblogging that

00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:19.450
precedes that, that might still give us
just a correlation rather than actual and

00:03:19.450 --> 00:03:24.770
impact of the microblogging,
in a causal sense, onto the sales numbers.

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:28.460
And there's a lot of reasons to
think that the, if you will,

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:31.940
more naive approaches tend
to overstate the effect.

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:35.750
So the incorrect measurement would tend
to go towards overstatement rather than

00:03:35.750 --> 00:03:38.530
understatement of the effect,
the reason being that

00:03:40.070 --> 00:03:44.170
probably the most sales reason is that
the actual cause of a relationship

00:03:44.170 --> 00:03:47.490
could be from sales to word of mouth
rather than the other way around.

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:49.250
And this might be misattributed.

00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:53.030
So it could be the case that
you sell the product a lot and

00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:56.840
because a lot of people bought it they
then go on social media and talk about it.

00:03:56.840 --> 00:03:59.340
So it's really the sales that
precede the word of mouth

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:02.320
rather than the other way around
which is what we want to establish.

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:07.080
Now in our case, we can rule that out
because the censorship event wasn't

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:10.650
based on sales numbers or anything,
it's really external to the system and

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:13.480
its impacts micro blogging activity and

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:16.790
we can then trace through
the effect of that on to sales.

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:19.720
And we can be sure that that
reduction microblogging

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:23.190
does not originate from sales differences.

00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:25.965
Which would be the case if we just compare
different products with each other.

00:04:25.965 --> 00:04:30.642
[MUSIC]

00:04:30.642 --> 00:04:35.202
So what we find is that relative
to the typical movement in

00:04:35.202 --> 00:04:39.762
TV ratings doubling the number,
doubling micro blogger

00:04:39.762 --> 00:04:44.521
activity moves it by about
20% of the typical movement.

00:04:44.521 --> 00:04:48.075
It happens from episode to episode,
which coming from just

00:04:48.075 --> 00:04:52.493
one advertising channel in this realm
is actually a fairly significant.

00:04:52.493 --> 00:04:54.700
The findings are significant in two ways.

00:04:56.050 --> 00:05:01.150
So one is, I think both intuitively for

00:05:01.150 --> 00:05:05.600
a lot of practitioners as well as in
the prior literature, academic literature,

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:08.200
we often thought these effects might
be even larger than what we find.

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:11.970
So to the extent that firms
are increasingly shifting their marketing

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:16.500
dollars into those channels it's very
useful to know that maybe these effects

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:21.410
might have been overstated and we have
to be careful in sort of managing them.

00:05:21.410 --> 00:05:25.470
Secondly it's a realm where it's just
incredibly hard to study these effects

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:28.110
because user dragged content isn't
under your control as a firm.

00:05:28.110 --> 00:05:30.990
So you can't manipulate it in order
to experiment around with it.

00:05:30.990 --> 00:05:35.230
So you actually have to resort to
methods like the one that we're using,

00:05:35.230 --> 00:05:37.650
which we call quasi-experimental.

00:05:37.650 --> 00:05:39.550
We're not doing the experiment ourselves,

00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:42.630
but we have something that is
almost like an experiment.

00:05:42.630 --> 00:05:45.050
The Chinese government,
if you will, experimented for

00:05:45.050 --> 00:05:47.060
us by shutting it down for three days.

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:49.950
They didn't mean to shut it down for
TV shows, but it so happened that it's

00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:53.630
something that We can leverage to
actually understand the affect.

00:05:53.630 --> 00:05:57.680
It's a study that cautions
a little bit against overstating.

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:00.880
That is sort of the magic bullet
in terms of marketing channels.

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:03.400
So it doesn't seem like it's clearly
out performing other channels.

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:06.934
So it seems to me if anything we still
want to stick with a certain mix of

00:06:06.934 --> 00:06:10.540
marketing tools, rather,
diverting everything into that realm.

00:06:10.540 --> 00:06:15.689
[MUSIC]

